## Rate Limiting

App-level rate limiting is **Flask-Limiter**, set up in `rate_limits.py` and
called from `app.py` after `Feather(__name__)` has registered the routes.
Feather's own `@rate_limit` decorator is *per process*: with `--workers 4` a
limit of 10/minute really allows 40 and a restart forgets the counters, so
use it only as a quick guard in development.

- The numbers are `RATELIMIT_DEFAULT`, `RATELIMIT_LOGIN` and
  `RATELIMIT_ADMIN` in `config.py`. `RATELIMIT_STORAGE_URI` (or `REDIS_URL`)
  points the counters at Redis; without it they are per-process memory.
- To limit a route that is already registered, put the wrapper back:
  `app.view_functions[endpoint] = limiter.limit(rule)(view)`. Calling
  `limiter.limit(...)` and discarding what it returns enforces nothing and
  silently drops the endpoint out of the default limit too.
- Static assets must never count against a limit - one page load fetches ten
  or more files through Flask. `rate_limits.EXEMPT_ENDPOINTS` covers `static`
  and `feather_static`; add to it, do not remove from it.
- New routes get the default limit automatically. Give a route its own limit
  with `@limiter.limit(...)` at definition time, or `_apply()` in
  `rate_limits.py` for one that already exists.

```bash
flask limiter limits      # every route Flask-Limiter actually limits
```

